[38;5;231m⏺[39m Next is where the filters go on the page, which is easier to judge by seeing it. I've opened a browser companion with
  three wireframes:

  [94m]8;id=sbkdzl;http://localhost:50141/?key=d6026fbcc550ce83ba07b966daae2b51a03336d6119f019e98e49c193d215029\http://localhost:50141/?key=d6026fbcc550ce83ba07b966daae2b51a03336d6119f019e98e49c193d215029[39m]8;;\

  - [1mA. Filter bar above the table:[0m every control is visible at once in a row that wraps on narrow screens.
  - [1mB. Quick filters plus a "More filters" popover:[0m "Failed sign-ins" and "Security changes" are one click away. Event
    type and dates sit in a popover, and active filters show as removable tags.
  - [1mC. Side column:[0m a checklist of all nine types and the date fields sit beside the table. This takes width away from
    the table.

  "Security changes" is a preset I added for what you described. It covers four of the nine existing types: Password
  changed, Two-factor enabled, Email change requested and Settings updated. It doesn't create a new type.

  I'd lean toward A. It needs the least JavaScript, has no popover focus handling to get right, and is the easiest to
  keep under the 300-line budget. B is the better choice if you want the default view as uncluttered as possible.

  Take a look and tell me here which works. You can also click an option in the browser.

  The companion saves its files under [38;5;153m.hyperpowers/[39m, which isn't in [38;5;153m.gitignore[39m yet. You may want to add it.

